# Mara loafer source review — A for one CPU build, with output inventory

The complete new source supports one separately root-admitted CPU entry. This is conditional on the explicit external output-inventory duty below. No build, target import, subdivision or viewer execution occurred in this review.

The upper is a literal connected quad patch with an entry hole, rather than the refused analytic loft with smoothing added (design 7–39; build 185–195). The fixed refinement uses face means, quarter-weight interior edge masks, manifold interior vertex weights and two-neighbor boundary weights consistent with the selected primary Catmark rules (build 56–78). Two steps, inward faces/bridges, the annular saddle's own-upper embedding, exact outer-loop/midsole contacts and named detail paths are explicit. The complete nine-part source owns its interfaces and exports; it does not prove global nonintersection, physical enclosure, fit or appearance.

**F1 — A failed write can leave an unlisted partial output.** Build 220 writes three exclusive files sequentially; 224–229 catches failures and separately attempts the result file. Either data or result writing can fail after creating a prefix. The result/output list therefore cannot establish the complete directory state. This is a source-supported evidence risk, not an observed failure or lost data.

Alternative A is the selected root-owned inventory on either actual exit: collect the owned entry, inspect the isolated flat output without following symlinks, cap it at eight entries/6 MiB plus per-file limits, preserve partials/unknowns, and close before model parsing or GPU on any mismatch. This gives independent actual evidence without changing source. Alternative B is to withhold this entry and defer producer-owned partial-path bookkeeping to another contract; that avoids the external duty but requires source changes unavailable in this first-refusal window. A is a mandatory admission condition, now explicit in ROOT-OUTPUT-PROTOCOL.json (d427bddb…); it must be bound in the CPU admission. It is not an assumption that the producer is exhaustive.

The new viewer uses the exact tested lifetime owner with retained host diagnostics, guarded fetch/body/hash/decode seams, registered synchronous resource creation/publication, child cleanup before Ready and both renderer release API attempts (viewer 25–60, 81–143). Its known 31 child and seven eventual host identities are bounded. Private constructor allocations, physical driver retirement and actual client success remain unqualified. Fixed cameras and the explicitly selected inside surfaces are declared before images; hypothetical box checks are not actual framing evidence.

All 50 input pins and six repeated source rows match; eight exact target paths are absent. The API excerpt reconstructs from the pinned installed sources, library copies are exact, and the saved primary header matches its raw response. Read-only clipping was recovered within the declared scope.

The 10-second check and 512 MiB RSS threshold are cooperative/sampled; the 128 MiB flag limits old space. Root must verify the stable pair/current pins and fresh admission before the sole CPU entry. Actual complete saved-data A and a fresh served-source/GPU gate remain necessary afterward. First failure/unknown closes without repair or retry. No geometry/appearance/fit/runtime/AAA or checklist acceptance follows source A. Only this pair was written; no pending edits.
